# Changes to AWS permissions, to the bot's own review gates, and to this file
# must be approved by a human.
# delegate[bot] is a GitHub App and cannot be a team member, so a code-owner
# requirement naming a team is structurally unsatisfiable by it.
#
# gp-contrib is used because it is the only team with write access to this
# repo; gp-secops has read only, and GitHub silently ignores code owners
# without write access.
#
# /delegate/ is the whole tree rather than just pr-reviewer.ts, because the
# reviewer's behaviour is not confined to one file: the framework sets its
# allowed tools, the worker swaps in the reviewer App's token and sets
# PR_REVIEWER_APPROVAL_ENABLED, and the subagent prompts drive what it looks
# at. Gating one file would leave the same hole one file over, and the
# existing gates are path-based rather than content-based for the same reason.
#
# /deploy-org/ provisions organizational units and member accounts. Creating
# an account is not a permission change, but it carries the same shape of
# risk: those resources are protected precisely because removing one detaches
# or closes a real account, and the role that applies them is the only one in
# the org holding organizations:CreateAccount.

/delegate/                             @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy-org/                           @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy/components/ci-roles/           @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy/components/ci-roles.ts         @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy/components/identity-center/    @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy/components/identity-center.ts  @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/deploy/deploy.sh                      @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
/.github/CODEOWNERS                    @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
